  <div style="margin-top: 40px;">
    <h2 class="section-title">Deductions Subject to the 2% Limit</h2>
    <div class="content-body">
      <p>You can deduct certain expenses as miscellaneous itemized deductions on Schedule A (Form 1040 or Form 1040NR). You can claim the amount of expenses that is more than 2% of your adjusted gross income. You figure out the amount of expenses that is more than 2% of your adjusted gross income from the total amount of these expenses. Your adjusted gross income is the amount on line 11 of Form 1040 or Form 1040NR.</p>
      <p>Generally, you apply the 2% limit after you apply any other deduction limit. For example, you apply the 50% limit on unreimbursed employee expenses before you apply the 2% limit on miscellaneous itemized deductions. You must keep records to verify your deductions.</p>
      <p><strong>Unreimbursed Employee Expenses</strong></p>
      <p>You may be able to deduct the following items as unreimbursed employee expenses:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="expense_type">{{ expense_type }}</span>.</li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>Business Bad Debt</strong></p>
      <p>A business bad debt is a loss from a debt created or incurred in your trade or business. Any other worthless debt is not a business bad debt.</p>
      <p><strong>Business Liability Insurance</strong></p>
      <p>You can deduct insurance premiums paid for protecting against personal liability for wrongful acts on the job.</p>
      <p><strong>Damages for Breach of Employment Contract</strong></p>
      <p>If you break an employment contract, you can deduct damages you pay your former employer if the damages are attributable to the breach.</p>
      <p><strong>Depreciation on Computers or Cell Phones</strong></p>
      <p>You can deduct depreciation for a computer or cell phone that you use in your work as an employee if it is used:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><span data-field="depreciation_condition">{{ depreciation_condition }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="depreciation_condition">{{ depreciation_condition }}</span>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>For the convenience of your employer.</strong> This means that your use of the computer or cell phone is for a substantial business reason of your employer. You must consider all facts in making this determination. See <span data-field="reference_section">{{ reference_section }}</span> for more information.</p>
      <p><strong>Dues to Chambers of Commerce and Professional Societies</strong></p>
      <p>You may be able to deduct dues paid to professional organizations (such as bar associations and medical associations) if the dues are directly related to your job. Similar organizations include:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><span data-field="organization_type">{{ organization_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="organization_type">{{ organization_type }}</span>.</li>
        <li><span data-field="organization_type">{{ organization_type }}</span>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Lobbying and political activities.</strong> You may not be able to deduct expenses for lobbying or political activities. See <span data-field="reference_section">{{ reference_section }}</span>.</p>
      <p><strong>Educator Expenses</strong></p>
      <p>If you were an eligible educator in 2009, you can deduct up to $300 of unreimbursed expenses for education. You can deduct expenses for books, supplies, equipment (including computer materials), and other materials used in your classroom. You can deduct expenses for professional development if you are a teacher or instructor, counselor, principal, or administrator. You can deduct expenses for classroom supplies, books, and other materials used in your classroom. You can deduct expenses for professional development if you are a teacher or instructor, counselor, principal, or administrator.</p>
      <p><strong>Qualified expenses.</strong> Qualified expenses include ordinary and necessary expenses paid in connection with books, supplies, equipment (including computer materials), and other materials used in your classroom. You can deduct expenses for professional development if you are a teacher or instructor, counselor, principal, or administrator.</p>
      <p><strong>Nontaxable qualified expenses.</strong> Nontaxable qualified expenses include ordinary and necessary expenses paid in connection with books, supplies, equipment (including computer materials), and other materials used in your classroom. You can deduct expenses for professional development if you are a teacher or instructor, counselor, principal, or administrator.</p>
    </div>
