  <div style="margin-top: 40px;">
    <div class="two-column">
      <div class="column">
        <h3 class="section-title">Your employer's business is generally for the convenience of your employer.</h3>
        <div class="content-body">
          <p><strong>Required as a condition of your employment.</strong> This means that you cannot perform your duties with out the computer or cell phone. Whether you can properly perform your duties with or without the computer or cell phone is not necessary that your employer must provide the computer or cell phone. It is not necessary that your employer must provide the computer or cell phone. It is not necessary that your employer must provide the computer or cell phone. It is not necessary that your employer must provide the computer or cell phone.</p>
          <p><strong>Example.</strong> You are an employee of a law firm. You occasionally take work home at night and at home. You use a computer to prepare documents for the law firm. You use the computer at your home. Since your employer requires you to use the computer to perform your job, the computer is for the convenience of your employer and is not required as a condition of your employment, you cannot deduct the expense.</p>
          <p><strong>Which depreciation method to use.</strong> The depreciation method you use depends on whether you meet the requirements for the business use of your home.</p>
          <p><strong>More-than-50% use test.</strong> You must meet this test if you use the computer or cell phone more than 50% in your work. If you meet this test, you can claim accelerated depreciation deduction for the year you place the item in service.</p>
          <p><strong>More-than-50% use test.</strong> You must meet this test if you use the computer or cell phone more than 50% in your work. If you meet this test, you can claim accelerated depreciation deduction for the year you place the item in service.</p>
          <p><strong>Investment use.</strong> Your use of a computer or cell phone in connection with investments (described later under Other Expenses) does not count as use in your work. You cannot claim depreciation on the computer or cell phone if you use it for investment purposes.</p>
          <p><strong>Exception for computer used in a home office.</strong> The more-than-50% use test does not apply to a computer used in a home office. You can claim accelerated depreciation deduction for the year you place the item in service. See <span data-field="reference_section">{{ reference_section }}</span> for more information.</p>
          <p><strong>More information.</strong> For more information on depreciation, see Publication 946. For more information on itemized deductions, see Publication 552. Publication 946 has detailed information about the section 179 deduction and depreciation deductions.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="column">
        <h3 class="section-title">Reporting your depreciation deduction.</h3>
        <div class="content-body">
          <p>See How To Report for information about reporting a deduction for depreciation.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
