</div>




<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">Page 9 of 24 of Publication 529</div>
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">11:27 - 5-NOV-2009</div>
  <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Clerical help and office rent in caring for investments.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Depreciation on home computers used for investments.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Excess deductions (including administrative ex-penses) allowed a beneficiary on termination of an estate or trust.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Fees to collect interest and dividends.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Hobby expenses, but generally not more than hobby income.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Indirect miscellaneous deductions of pass-through entities.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Investment fees and expenses.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Legal fees related to producing or collecting taxable income or getting tax advice.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Loss on deposits in an insolvent or bankrupt finan-cial institution.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Loss on traditional IRAs or Roth IRAs, when all amounts have been distributed to you.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Repayments of income.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Repayments of social security benefits.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Safe deposit box rental.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Service charges on dividend reinvestment plans.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Tax advice fees.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Fees's fees for your IRA, if separately billed and not reimbursed.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Clerical Help and Office Rent</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You can deduct office expenses, such as rent and clerical help, that you have in connection with your investments and collecting the taxable income on them.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Credit or Debit Card Convenience Fees</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You can deduct the convenience fee charged by the card processor for paying your income tax (including estimated tax payments) by credit or debit card. The fees are deducti-ble in the year paid. For example, fees charged to pay-ments made in 2009 can be claimed on the 2009 tax return.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Depreciation on Home Computer</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You can deduct depreciation on your home computer if you use it to produce income (for example, to manage your investments to produce taxable income). You must meet the computer usage the straight line method over the Alternative Depreciation System (ADS) and you must use it for more than 50% of the time in the computer that work, see Depreciation and Amortization, or other rules under Unreimbursed Employee Ex-penses. Earlier, for more information on depreciation, see Publication 946.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">Page 10 of 24 of Publication 529</div>
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">11:27 - 5-NOV-2009</div>
  <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Indirect Deductions of Pass-Through Entities</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Pass-through entities include partnerships, s corpora-tions, and mutual funds that are not publicly offered. De-ductions of pass-through entities are generally passed through to the partners or shareholders. The partners or shareholders are responsible for reporting these deductions on their personal tax returns, subject to the rules of the entity. The entity itself does not take the deduction.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Example. You are a member of an investment club that is formed solely to invest in securities. The club is treated as a partnership. The partnership's income is solely from investment income. The partnership's deductions are also solely from investment deductions. The partnership does not take the deduction; you do.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Publicly offered mutual funds.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Publicly offered mutual funds do not pass deductions for investment expenses to shareholders. A mutual fund is "publicly offered" if it is:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 0.9em; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li>Regularly offered pursuant to a public offering, ket, or</li>
        <li>Held or for at or lost sales of a partnership during the tax year.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Excess Deductions of an Estate</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">If an estate's total deductions in its last tax year are more than its gross income for that year, the beneficiaries suc-ceeding to the estate's property can deduct the excess. Do not include deductions for the estate's personal exemption or the estate's deductions for carrying over losses from prior years. The deduction is limited to the amount of the estate's gross income for the tax year in which, or with which, the estate termi-nates, whichever is the greater. For more information, see Termination of Estate in Publication 559, Survivors, Executors, and Ad-ministrators.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Fees To Collect Interest and Dividends</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You can deduct fees you pay to a broker, bank, trustee, or similar agent to collect your taxable interest or divi-dends on shares of stock. But you cannot deduct a fee you pay to a broker to buy investment property, such as stocks or bonds. You must add the fee to the cost of the property. For more information, see Publication 559.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Hobby Expenses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You can generally deduct hobby expenses, but only up to the amount of hobby income. A hobby is not a business</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">Page 11 of 24 of Publication 529</div>
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">11:27 - 5-NOV-2009</div>
  <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Repayments of Income</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">If you had to repay an amount that you included in income in an earlier year, you may be able to deduct the amount you repay. If the amount you repay is less than the amount you originally included in income, you can deduct the difference. If the amount you repay is more than the amount you originally included in income, you cannot deduct the excess. For more information, see Publication 590, Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs).</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Repayments of Social Security Benefits</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">If the total of the amounts in box 5 (net benefits for 2009) of your Form SSA-1099, and the amounts in box 1 of your Form SSA-1099, are greater than the amount of benefits you received in 2009, you may be able to take a miscellaneous itemized deduction for the amount of the repayment. This deduction is subject to the 2% limit. The amount in box 1 of your Form SSA-1099 is the amount of benefits you received in 2009. The amount in box 5 of your Form SSA-1099 is the amount of benefits you repaid in 2009 (box 4) is more than the gross amounts of benefit paid to you in 2009 (box 1).</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Investment Fees and Expenses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You can deduct investment fees, custodial fees, trust ad-ministration fees, and other expenses you pay for manag-ing your investments that produce taxable income.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Legal Expenses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You can usually deduct legal expenses that you incur in attempting to produce or collect taxable income or that you pay in connection with the determination, collection, or refund of any tax.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You can also deduct legal expenses that are:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 0.9em; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li>Related to doing anything your job, such as defending yourself against criminal charges arising out of your trade or business.</li>
        <li>For tax advice related to a divorce if the bill specifies that the advice is for tax advice and it is determined in a reasonable way, or</li>
        <li>To collect taxable alimony.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Loss on Deposits</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">If you can reasonably estimate the amount of your losses on deposits in a financial institution that becomes insolvent or bankrupt, you can generally choose to deduct it in the current year even though its exact amount has not been finally determined. Once you make this election, you cannot change it without incurring a penalty. If none of the deposit is federally insured, you can deduct the loss in either of the following ways:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 0.9em; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li>As a miscellaneous itemized deduction subject to the 2% limit. Write the name of the financial institu-tion on Schedule A (Form 1040), line 23, or Schedule A (Form 1040NR), line 11. This deduction is subject to the 2% limit. If you are an employee, you must file a separate form for each financial institution, as deducted by any expected state insurance proceeds.</li>
        <li>As a casualty loss. See Publication 547 for details.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">Page 12 of 24 of Publication 529</div>
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">11:27 - 5-NOV-2009</div>
  <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Amortizable Premium on Taxable Bonds</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">In general, if the amount you pay for a bond is greater than its stated principal amount, the excess is bond premium. You can elect to amortize the premium on taxable bonds. The amortization of the premium is generally an offset to interest income on the bond rather than a separate deduc-tion.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Pre-1998 election to amortize bond premium.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">If you elected to amortize bond premium before 1998, the amortization of the premium is treated as a deduction. If you did not elect to amortize bond premium before 1998, the amortization of the premium is treated as an offset to interest income.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Bonds acquired after October 22, 1986, and before January 1, 1999.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">The amortization of the premium on these bonds is treated as an offset to interest income. You must elect to amortize the premium on these bonds by the due date of your tax return. If you do not elect to amortize the premium, the premium is treated as a bond premium deduction on the bond in prior periods. If any of the excess bond premium cannot be deducted because of the limit, this amount is carried for forward to the next tax year. For more information, see Publication 550.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Pre-1998 choice to amortize bond premium.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">If you made the choice to amortize the premium on the bond before 1998, the amortization of the premium is treated as a deduction. If you did not make the choice to amortize the premium before 1998, the amortization of the premium is treated as an offset to interest income.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">More information.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">For more information on bond pre-mium, see Bond Premium Amortization in chapter 3 of Publication 550.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Federal Estate Tax on Income in Respect of a Decedent</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You can deduct the federal estate tax attributable to in-come in respect of a decedent that you as a beneficiary received. If the decedent was subject to the estate tax, the income in respect of a decedent would have been subject to the estate tax. If the decedent was not subject to the estate tax, the income in respect of a decedent would not have been subject to the estate tax. For more information, see Publication 559 for information on the deduction.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Gambling Losses Up to the Amount of Gambling Winnings</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You must report the full amount of your gambling winnings on your tax return. You can deduct gambling losses up to the amount of your gambling winnings. Generally, nonresident aliens cannot deduct gambling losses on Schedule A (Form 1040NR).</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot reduce your gambling winnings by losses from other sources. You must report the full amount of your winnings as income and claim your losses (up to the amount of your winnings) as an itemized deduction. Therefore, your net gambling loss should show your winnings separately from your losses.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Diary of winnings and losses.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You should keep a diary of your winnings and losses. You should also have other documentation. You can gen-erally prove your winnings and losses through Form W-2, certain gambling winnings, Form 574, Statement by the casino, and other documentation. You must keep a record of your winnings and losses.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">For specific wagering transactions, you can use the following forms to support your winnings and losses:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 0.9em; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><span data-field="gambling_forms_used">{{ gambling_forms_used }}</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">Page 13 of 24 of Publication 529</div>
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">11:27 - 5-NOV-2009</div>
  <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Keno</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Copies of the keno tickets you purchased that were validated by the gambling establishment, copies of your casino credit records, and copies of your casino cash receipts.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Slot machines.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">A record of the machine number and all winnings by date and time. You should also keep a record of the table at which you were playing. Casino credit records and cash receipts are also helpful.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Bingo.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">A record of the number of games played, cost of tickets, and winnings. You should also keep a record of the casino, patio, etc.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Racing (horse, harness, dog, etc.).</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">A record of the race, the name of the horse, the track, the date, the amount wagered, and the amount won. You should also keep a record of the racetrack.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Lotteries.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">A record of ticket purchases, dates, win-nings, and losses. Supplement records include winnings statements.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Impairment-Related Work Expenses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">If you have a physical or mental disability that limits your ability to work, you may be able to deduct expenses related to your impairment. These expenses must be directly related to your impairment and not to your general medical care. You must keep records of these expenses.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Loss From Other Activities From Schedule K-1 (Form 1065-B), Box 2</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">If the amount reported in Schedule K-1 (Form 1065-B), box 2, is a loss, report it on Schedule A (Form 1040), line 28, or Schedule A (Form 1040NR), line 16 only if effectively connected with a U.S. trade or business. It is not subject to the 2% limit.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Officials Paid on a Fee Basis</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">If you are a fee-basis official, you can claim your expenses in performing services in that job as an adjustment to income, subject to the 2% limit.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Performing Artists</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">If you are a qualified performing artist, you can deduct your employee business expenses as an adjustment to income, subject to the 2% limit. You must keep records of your expenses.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Repayments Under Claim of Right</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">If you had to repay more than $3,000 that you included in income in an earlier year because of a claim of right, you may be able to deduct the amount you repay, or take a credit against your tax. See Publication 525 for more information.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Unrecovered Investment in Annuity</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">A retiree who contributed to the cost of an annuity can deduct the amount of the unrecovered investment in the annuity. This deduction is limited to the amount of the unrecovered investment. For more information about the tax treat-ment of pensions and annuities.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Nondeductible Expenses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct the following expenses:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 0.9em; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><span data-field="nondeductible_expense_1">{{ nondeductible_expense_1 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="nondeductible_expense_2">{{ nondeductible_expense_2 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="nondeductible_expense_3">{{ nondeductible_expense_3 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="nondeductible_expense_4">{{ nondeductible_expense_4 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="nondeductible_expense_5">{{ nondeductible_expense_5 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="nondeductible_expense_6">{{ nondeductible_expense_6 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="nondeductible_expense_7">{{ nondeductible_expense_7 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="nondeductible_expense_8">{{ nondeductible_expense_8 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="nondeductible_expense_9">{{ nondeductible_expense_9 }}</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">Page 14 of 24 of Publication 529</div>
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">11:27 - 5-NOV-2009</div>
  <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Health spa expenses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Hobby losses—but see Hobby expenses, earlier.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Home repairs, insurance, and rent.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Home security system.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Illegal bribes and kickbacks—see Bribes and kick-backs in chapter 11 of Publication 535.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Life insurance premiums.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Lobbying expenses.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Losses from the sale of your home, furniture, per-sonal car, etc.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Lunches with co-workers.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Meals while working late.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Personal disability insurance premiums.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Personal legal expenses.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Personal, living, or family expenses.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Political contributions.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Professional accreditation fees.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Professional reputation, expenses to improve.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Residential telephone line.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Stockholders' meeting, expenses of attending.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">The value of wages never received or lost vacation time.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Travel expenses for another individual.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Voluntary unemployment benefit fund contributions.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Wristwatches.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Capital Expenses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot currently deduct amounts paid to buy property that has a useful life extending beyond the tax year or property that is used in your work, you may be able to take a depreciation deduction. See Publication 946.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Check-Writing Fees on Personal Account</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">If you have a personal checking account, you cannot deduct fees charged by the bank for the privilege of writing checks, even if the account pays interest.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Club Dues</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Generally, you cannot deduct the cost of membership in any club organized for business, pleasure, recreation, or other social purpose. You can deduct the cost of membership in a club if the club is organized for business purposes.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct dues paid to an organization if one of its main purposes is to:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 0.9em; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><span data-field="club_purpose_1">{{ club_purpose_1 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="club_purpose_2">{{ club_purpose_2 }}</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Dues paid to airline, hotel, and luncheon clubs are not deductible.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Commuting Expenses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct commuting expenses (the cost of trans-portation between your home and your main or regular place of work). If you haul tools, instruments, or other items to your work, you may be able to deduct the cost of transportation for those items, such as the rent on a trailer to carry the items.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Fines or Penalties</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct fines or penalties you pay to a govern-ment. This includes parking tickets, traffic tickets, and penalties deducted from taxpayers' paychecks after an illegal strike.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Health Spa Expenses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct health spa expenses, even if there is a job requirement to stay in excellent physical condition, such as might be required of a law enforcement officer.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">Page 15 of 24 of Publication 529</div>
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">11:27 - 5-NOV-2009</div>
  <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Lobbying Expenses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You generally cannot deduct amounts paid or incurred for lobbying expenses. These include expenses to:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 0.9em; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><span data-field="lobbying_expense_1">{{ lobbying_expense_1 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="lobbying_expense_2">{{ lobbying_expense_2 }}</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Lobbying expenses also include any amounts paid or incurred for research, preparation, planning, or coordina-tion of any of these activities.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Covered executive branch official.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">A covered executive branch official is an employee of the President or an officer of the Executive Office of the President, and the two most senior level officers of each of the other agen-cies of the Executive Branch.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Any individual serving in a position in Level I of the Executive Branch, or any other individual designated by the President as having Cabinet-level status, and any immediate deputy of one of these individuals.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Dues used for lobbying.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">If a tax-exempt organization notifies you that part of the dues or other amounts you pay</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Lost or Mislaid Cash or Property</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct a loss based on the mere disappear-ance of money or property. However, an accidental loss or misplacement of property can qualify as a casualty if it meets the requirements of a casualty loss. See Publication 547.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Lunches With Co-workers</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct the expenses of lunches with co-workers, except while traveling away from home on business. See Publication 463 for information on deducti-ble expenses while traveling away from home.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Meals While Working Late</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct the cost of meals while working late. However, you may be able to claim a deduction if the cost of the meal is a deductible entertainment expense, or if you are traveling away from home. See Publication 463 for information on deductible expenses and ex-penses while traveling away from home.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Personal Legal Expenses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct personal legal expenses such as those for:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 0.9em; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><span data-field="personal_legal_expense_1">{{ personal_legal_expense_1 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="personal_legal_expense_2">{{ personal_legal_expense_2 }}</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">Page 16 of 24 of Publication 529</div>
  <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 10px;">11:27 - 5-NOV-2009</div>
  <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Civil or criminal charges resulting from a personal relationship.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Damages for personal injury (except certain physical injuries) and libel or slander. You may be able to deduct these damages if they are related to your trade or business. See Publication 535.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Preparation of a title (or defense or perfection of a property claim).</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You may be able to deduct these expenses if they are related to your trade or business.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Property claims or property settlement in a divorce.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You may be able to deduct these expenses if they are related to your trade or business.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Political Contributions</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct contributions made to a political candi-date, a campaign committee, or a newsletter fund. Adver-tising for a candidate or a political party or political candidate are not deductible.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Professional Accreditation Fees</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You can deduct professional accreditation fees such as the following:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 0.9em; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><span data-field="accreditation_fee_1">{{ accreditation_fee_1 }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="accreditation_fee_2">{{ accreditation_fee_2 }}</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Professional Reputation</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct expenses of radio and TV appearances to increase your personal prestige or establish your profes-sional reputation.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Relief Fund Contributions</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct contributions paid to a private plan that pays benefits to any covered employee who cannot work because of any injury or illness not related to the job.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Residential Telephone Service</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct any charge (including taxes) for basic local telephone service if it is used in a trade or business.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Stockholders' Meetings</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct transportation and other expenses you pay to attend stockholders' meetings of companies in</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Which you own stock but have no other interest. You cannot deduct these expenses even if you are attending meetings to get information that would be useful in making further investments.</h3>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Tax-Exempt Income Expenses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct expenses to produce tax-exempt in-come. You cannot deduct interest on a debt incurred or continued to buy or carry tax-exempt securities.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">Example. During the year, you received taxable inter-est of $5,400 and tax-exempt interest of $1,200. In earning the taxable interest, you incurred expenses of $500. In earning the tax-exempt interest, you incurred expenses of $100. You can deduct the expenses of $500 for the taxable interest and 20% of the expenses of $100 for the tax-exempt interest. You can deduct $500, subject to the 2% limit, expenses of $400 (90% of $100).</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Travel Expenses for Another Individual</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You generally cannot deduct travel expenses you pay or incur for another individual. You can deduct travel expenses for another individual if the individual is an employee or a contractor, and you are paying for the travel as part of your job duties. See Publication 463 for more information on deductible travel expenses.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Voluntary Unemployment Benefit Fund Contributions</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct voluntary unemployment benefit fund contributions. You cannot deduct contributions to a union fund or a private fund. You can deduct contributions to a union fund only if the union fund is organized for business purposes.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">Wristwatches</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You cannot deduct the cost of a wristwatch, even if there is a job requirement that you know the correct time to prop-erly perform your duties.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 20px;">How To Report</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You must itemize deductions on Schedule A (Form 1040 or Form 1040NR) to claim miscellaneous deductions subject to the 2% limit. You must also report your itemized deductions on line 24 for Form 1040, or lines 9 through 11 and the total on line 12 for Form 1040NR.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>


