  </div>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin-bottom: 30px;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <p style="font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px;">• Report your miscellaneous deductions not subject to the 2% limit on line 28 for Form 1040, or line 16 for Form 1040NR.</p>
      <p style="margin-top: 15px;">See <span data-field="reference_link_1">{{ reference_link_1 }}</span> booklet for more information.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <p style="font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px;">Armed Forces reservists. If you have reserve-related travel that takes you more than 100 miles from home, you should first complete Form 2106 or Form 2106-EZ. See Publication 463 for more information.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px;">Form 2106 and Form 2106-EZ. If you have deductible employee business expenses, you usually must file either Form 2106 or Form 2106-EZ.</p>
    <p style="margin-bottom: 10px;">You must file Form 2106 or Form 2106-EZ if any of the following applies to you:</p>
    <ol style="margin-top: 0;">
      <li>You are a qualified performing artist claiming per-forming artist-related expenses.</li>
      <li>You are a fee-based state or local government official claiming expenses in performing that job.</li>
      <li>You are a member of the armed forces and disability are claim-ing work-related expenses. See <span data-field="reference_link_2">{{ reference_link_2 }}</span>.</li>
      <li>You have travel expenses as a member of the Armed Forces reserved that you can deduct as an entertainment expense.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px;">Depreciation. Use Part V of Form 4562, Depreciation and Amortization, to claim the depreciation deduction for a cell phone or for a computer that you did not use only in your home office. Complete Form 4562, Part II, if you are claim-ing a deduction for a cell phone. However, if you are claim-ing Form 2106 or Form 2106-EZ, claim your depreciation deduction on that form and not on Form 4562.</p>
    <p style="margin-top: 15px;">Computer used in a home office. Use Form 4562, Part II, to claim the depreciation deduction for a computer that you used only in your home office. Complete Form 4562, Part I, if you are claim-ing a deduction for a cell phone.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px;">Do not use Form 4562 to claim the depreciation deduction for a cell phone. If you receive a cell phone in 2000 and used only in your home office, unless you are otherwise required to file Form 4562, instead, report the depreciation directly on the appropriate form. But if you are otherwise required to file Form 4562, report the depreciation in Part II.</p>
    <p style="margin-top: 15px;">Important related work expenses. If you are an em-ployee, you can claim certain work expenses on Form 2106 or Form 2106-EZ. Enter on Schedule A (Form 1040), line 28, or Schedule A (Form 1040NR), line 16, that part of the amount on Form 2106, line 10, or Form 2106-EZ, line 10 that is related to your employment. Enter the amount that is related to your employment. Enter the amount that is not related to your employment on Schedule A (Form 1040), line 21, or Schedule A (Form 1040NR), line 21.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <p style="font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px;">Who can use Form 2106-EZ. You can use the shorter Form 2106-EZ instead of Form 2106 if both of the following apply:</p>
    <ol style="margin-top: 0;">
      <li>You are not reimbursed by your employer for any expenses. (Amounts your employer included as expenses on Form W-2, box 1, are not considered reimbursed.)</li>
      <li>Either:
        <ul style="margin-top: 5px; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li>You are not claiming vehicle expense, or</li>
          <li>You are using the standard mileage rate for your vehicle.</li>
        </ul>
    </ol>
  </div>
</div>

